Chapter: RocRecursiveVar
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: recursive function with var keeps outer binding":

     {
         f = |n| {
             var $state = n
             if n > 0 {
                 inner = f(n - 1)
                 $state + inner
             } else {
                 $state
             }
         }
         f(3)
     }

 and Roc expects 6.

 Two adaptations. Roc's decimal default spells its answer `6.0`. And Roc's
 `var $state` is a MUTABLE local; Codex has no such thing, so `state` is an
 ordinary `let`.

 **That second one takes a real property out and leaves a real property in,
 and the difference is the whole reason this case is worth porting.** What
 is gone: nothing here mutates, so Roc's question -- does a var survive a
 recursive call into the same function -- cannot be asked. What is left is
 the half we can ask and the half our findings live in: `state` is bound
 BEFORE the recursive call and read AFTER it returns, in a frame the callee
 re-entered. Finding 42 was exactly that read going to the wrong place -- a
 self-tail loop that read a top-level definition where the source read its
 own parameter -- and it was found by zig noticing an unused parameter
 rather than by any row asking for it.

 `f 3` walks three frames deep and each one holds a live binding across its
 callee, so a rename or a loop variable that leaks between frames is off by
 more than one: 3 + 2 + 1 + 0.

Section: Functions

  f : Integer -> Integer
  f (n) =
   let state = n
   in if n > 0 then let inner = f (n - 1) in state + inner
      else state

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (f 3))
  end
